%[text] # comp.ClosedBox Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Closed-box enclosure: the rear of the diaphragm works on a sealed volume, the front radiates.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** comp.Enclosure
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] The `comp.ClosedBox` class models a closed-box enclosure. The rear of the diaphragm of the driver in `Driver` works on the air in a sealed volume `RearVolume`; the front of the diaphragm radiates into a half space, as a rigid piston in an infinite baffle. `lspsys` uses the enclosure in the acoustical side of its network, see [solve2PortNetwork](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))). The symbols are listed in [Symbols](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical model
%[text] The acoustical side of the system is the two-port network $ T\_a $, which holds the 4-port $ T\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ T\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation on three conductors: the front of the diaphragm (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear of the diaphragm (bottom). For a closed box:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:c1b0)
%[text] - $ T\_{a,e} $: the air in the box is the acoustic compliance $ C\_{a,cb,r} = \\frac{V\_b}{\\rho c^2} $ between the rear conductor and the ambient pressure, with the impedance $ Z\_{a,cb,r} = \\frac{1}{j \\omega C\_{a,cb,r}} $ (`zacbr`). The front conductor passes through.
%[text] - $ T\_{a,rad} $: the front of the diaphragm radiates through the radiation impedance $ Z\_{a,rad,f} $ (`zaradf`), between the front conductor and the ambient pressure. The radiation impedance is complex and is drawn as a box, as in Beranek. The rear does not radiate: the rear conductor passes through. \
%[text] The radiation impedance of a rigid piston with the radius $ r\_d $ in an infinite baffle, with the Bessel function $ J\_1 $ and the Struve function $ H\_1 $, is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ Z\_{a,rad,f} = \\frac{\\rho c}{\\pi r\_d^2} \\left( 1 - \\frac{J\_1(2 k r\_d)}{k r\_d} + j \\frac{H\_1(2 k r\_d)}{k r\_d} \\right) $
%[text] Both elements are shunts to the ambient pressure. With the flows to the right on the front conductor and to the left on the rear conductor, the 4 × 4 transmission matrices are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ T\_{a,e} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{Z\_{a,cb,r}} & 1} \\right\], \\quad T\_{a,rad} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\cr \\frac{1}{Z\_{a,rad,f}} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 0 & 0 & 1} \\right\] $
%[text] The minus sign follows from the direction of the rear flows. With the method of `solve2PortNetwork`, these matrices give the two-port network
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ T\_a = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 \\cr \\frac{1}{Z\_{a,rad,f} + Z\_{a,cb,r}} & 1} \\right\] $
%[text] so that, with port 2 open, $ p\_1 = (Z\_{a,rad,f} + Z\_{a,cb,r}) U\_d $: the radiation impedance and the compliance of the box in series, as in the classic closed-box circuit. All volume velocity of the diaphragm flows through $ Z\_{a,rad,f} $, so the radiated volume velocity is $ U\_r = U\_d $.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] obj = comp.ClosedBox
%[text] ```
%[text] Creates a closed box with a rear volume of 10 L and a default driver, `comp.Driver`, which has all its parameters set to zero. Set the parameters of the driver before you use the enclosure.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] The properties are listed in the order of the class definition files.
%%
%[text] ### `RearVolume` — Volume of the rear chamber
%[text] `(1,1) double` \[m3\]
%[text] Volume $ V\_b $ of the sealed chamber behind the diaphragm. Must be positive. Default: 10e-3 m3 (10 L).
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `Driver` — Driver in the enclosure
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Driver`
%[text] Driver that is mounted in the enclosure. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. Default: `comp.Driver`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `zaradf` | Instance | Radiation impedance $ Z\_{a,rad,f} $ at the front of the diaphragm, for frequencies `f` and radiation angle `ra`. |
%[text] | `zacbr` | Instance | Acoustic impedance $ Z\_{a,cb,r} $ of the closed volume at the rear, for frequencies `f`. |
%[text] | `tae` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ T\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure, with the closed volume $ Z\_{a,cb,r} $ as a shunt on the rear conductor. |
%[text] | `tarad` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ T\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, with $ Z\_{a,rad,f} $ as a shunt on the front conductor. |
%[text] | `diaphragm2RadiatedVolumeVelocity` | Instance | Ratio of the radiated to the diaphragm volume velocity; 1 for a closed box. |
%[text] | `zarad` | Static | Radiation impedance of a rigid circular piston in an infinite baffle. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `struve` | Static | Approximation of the Struve function $ H\_1 $. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Impedances of the front and the rear
%[text] Create a driver according to the specifications of a B&C 21SW152-8, mount it in a closed box of 200 liter, and plot the magnitude of the radiation impedance and of the impedance of the rear volume.
d = comp.Driver;
d.Re = 6;
d.Le = 2.2e-3;
d.Qes = 0.38;
d.Qms = 6.4;
d.Fs = 32;
d.Sd = 1680e-4;
d.Vas = 200e-3;
e = comp.ClosedBox;
e.RearVolume = 200e-3;
e.Driver = d;
f = logspace(log10(20),log10(20e3),1000);
loglog(f,abs(e.zaradf(f,"2pi")),f,abs(e.zacbr(f)),LineWidth=1.5)
grid on
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]")
ylabel("Acoustic impedance [Pa s/m^3]")
legend("Z_{a,rad,f}","Z_{a,cb,r}")
title("Impedances of the closed box")

